Each bug with its verified fix:

```tsx
// 1. SIBLINGS OUT OF SYNC — each had a copy; LIFT to the common parent
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');          // ONE owner
  return (
    <>
      <Search value={query} onChange={setQuery} />   {/* props down */}
      <Results query={query} />                      {/* same source — cannot drift */}
    </>
  );
}

// 2. TODO DIDN'T UPDATE — mutation kept the reference
setTodos(todos.push(item));          // WRONG: push returns a NUMBER, same array
setTodos([...todos, item]);          // RIGHT: new array reference → Object.is differs → render
setTodos(t => [...t, item]);         // BEST: functional form — no stale-closure risk

// 3. HANDLER FIRES DURING RENDER — you CALLED it
<button onClick={save()}>Save</button>       // WRONG: save() runs during render
<button onClick={save}>Save</button>           // RIGHT: pass the function
<button onClick={() => save(row.id)}>Save</button>  // with args: wrap in an arrow

// 4. SEARCH SHOWS OLD RESULTS — a race; guard by epoch or abort
const epoch = useRef(0);
async function search(q: string) {
  const mine = ++epoch.current;
  const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${q}`);
  if (mine !== epoch.current) return;          // a newer call superseded mine — discard
  setResults(await res.json());                // only the NEWEST result renders
}
```

The reasoning per bug (the interview structure):
1. Two components each owning the same fact = two truths — they WILL diverge. One owner (lowest common parent), props down, events up. If the parent is far, a store or context owns it — never two copies.
2. React's render gate is reference equality on state — the array you mutated IS the last render's array; nothing changed as far as the diff can tell. Immutable updates create a new reference; the functional form also protects you when updates batch and the outer variable is stale.
3. JSX attributes are EXPRESSIONS evaluated during render — save() with parens is evaluated the moment React builds the element. The value of onClick must be a function that React calls LATER on the actual click.
4. Networks don't order responses — a slow query 1 resolving after fast query 2 wins the race and paints stale data. The guard pattern (ignore responses whose ticket isn't current) makes the LAST request the only one that can render. AbortController additionally stops the work entirely (and aborts fetches, saves bandwidth).
